// src/parser.pdx — libpdx-argv.M1-001 + M1-002 + M2-001 + M2-002 + M2-003
//               + ENH-031 (argv[0]-skip wrapper)
//               + ENH-032 (--version auto-emit dispatch, Closes #25)
//               + ENH-014 (--help auto-table dispatch, Closes #24)
//               + ENH-013 (clustered short-flag expansion for BOOL/
//                          COUNTED, Closes #23)
//               + ENH-017 (collect-all-errors via parse_argv_ex +
//                          ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS parse_flags bit,
//                          Closes #27)
//               + ENH-019 (STRING flag enum validation via
//                          register_string_enum + post-store
//                          allowed-set walk, Closes #29)
//               + ENH-020 (git-shape subcommand dispatch via
//                          FlagSpec::register_subcommands +
//                          pre-loop dispatch phase, Closes #30)
// Parser: entry points parse_argv_ex(argv, argc, parse_flags) →
//         error_code (the ENH-017 primary), parse_argv(argv, argc) →
//         error_code (thin wrapper passing parse_flags = 0 — the
//         pre-ENH-017 signature preserved for every existing consumer,
//         see ENH-017 note below), and parse_argv_skipping_zero(argv,
//         argc) → error_code (thin wrapper that drops argv[0] before
//         forwarding to parse_argv).
//
// M1 grammar (unchanged interpretation, except ENH-013 for clusters):
//   long flags  : --foo, --foo=bar, --foo bar
//   short flags : one letter per hyphen (-f); clustered forms admitted
//                 IFF every letter's registration is FKIND_BOOL or (under
//                 permissive mode) FKIND_UNKNOWN — see ENH-013 below
//   positional  : any arg not classified as a flag
//   sentinel    : --pdx-schema sets emit_schema=1 (libpdx-argv debug)
//
// M2 additions:
//   M2-001 typed flags — Parser calls FlagSpec::lookup(name) to
//     decide whether to consume argv[i+1] as a value. Boolean and
//     unregistered flags NEVER consume; typed flags ALWAYS consume
//     (error if none available). Fixes the cat-M1 blocker where
//     `cat -n foo.txt` incorrectly bound `foo.txt` to `-n`.
//   M2-002 standard vocabulary — parser writes ParsedArgs::flag_ids[k]
//     and flag_kinds[k] alongside the raw pointer so consumers can
//     dispatch by ID rather than name compare. See StdVocab.
//   M2-003 positional-argument list — `--` sentinel handling. Once
//     `--` is seen at argv[i], every argv[j > i] is treated as a
//     positional regardless of its first byte. The parser also
//     accepts `:` as a value separator (in addition to `=`) so
//     `--no-cap:KIND_TTY` parses as name="no-cap", value="KIND_TTY".
//
// libpdx-argv.ENH-004 (opt-in strict mode, post-1.0):
//   FlagSpec::strict_mode defaults to 0 (permissive; unchanged
//   behaviour). When a consumer calls FlagSpec::set_strict(1), any
//   flag (long or short) whose name FlagSpec::lookup can't find fails
//   the parse with ParsedArgs::ERR_UNKNOWN_FLAG (12) and
//   error_arg_index = the offending argv index, instead of silently
//   storing it as a boolean. SchemaInvoke::parse_from_schema_record
//   applies the identical policy on its own invocation path.
//
// libpdx-argv.ENH-032 (--version auto-emit, Closes #25):
//   After every long-flag or single-letter short-flag store,
//   parse_argv checks whether the just-stored id equals
//   StdVocab::STD_ID_VERSION (2) AND VersionBackend::override_enabled
//   == 0. On both hits it calls VersionBackend::emit_default (which
//   writes the fingerprint `<tool> <ver>\n<TOOL> VERSION OK\n` to fd
//   1 via seven raw sys_writes) and returns ERR_VERSION_EMITTED (13)
//   via parse_argv_fail — the same failure return path every other
//   ERR_* code takes, so error_code and error_arg_index are recorded
//   uniformly. The check is by id, not by name compare: a tool that
//   registers --version via StdVocab::register_all gets the
//   auto-emit; a tool that does not register --version at all (or
//   that registers it with a non-2 id) never triggers it. Consumers
//   that want to render their own --version output opt out via
//   VersionBackend::set_override(1) at bootstrap.
//
// libpdx-argv.ENH-013 (clustered short-flag expansion, Closes #23):
//   M1-002 locked a one-per-hyphen short-flag grammar: any argv slot
//   whose byte0='-' and had two-or-more non-NUL bytes after the '-'
//   was rejected with ERR_CLUSTERED_SHORT (4). ENH-013 relaxes the
//   grammar for the mainstream cluster idiom (`-vv` for verbosity,
//   `-abc` for three switches) while preserving the D3 guarantee that
//   a value-consuming short flag never silently swallows one of its
//   cluster-mates: when EVERY letter in `-abc...` looks up as
//   FKIND_BOOL (0), OR — under permissive mode — as FKIND_UNKNOWN
//   (0xFF; the parser already treats these as boolean per M2-001),
//   the cluster is expanded into N independent short-flag stores
//   (flag_names[k] point at a per-letter NUL-terminated string in a
//   parser-owned scratch buffer, flag_values[k] = 0, flag_ids[k] and
//   flag_kinds[k] set from the lookup return). Any letter whose
//   lookup returns a value-consuming kind (FKIND_STR / FKIND_INT /
//   FKIND_TIMESPAN / FKIND_SIZE / FKIND_ENUM) fails the whole
//   cluster with ERR_CLUSTER_WITH_ARITY (16) BEFORE any letter is
//   dispatched — no partial stores. Strict mode (ENH-004) still
//   dominates: an unregistered letter under FlagSpec::set_strict(1)
//   fails with ERR_UNKNOWN_FLAG (12), same as the single-letter path.
//
//   Implementation shape: two-pass. Pass 1 walks the cluster once
//   using a 2-byte probe scratch (cluster_probe) as the NUL-terminated
//   1-char name string handed to FlagSpec::lookup, classifying each
//   letter (fail-fast on the first offending kind). Pass 2 walks the
//   cluster again, this time writing letter+NUL into a per-slot pair
//   inside cluster_scratch_buf keyed off flag_count*2, calling
//   FlagSpec::lookup again to recover (kind, id), and storing into
//   flag_names/flag_values/flag_ids/flag_kinds via the same MAX_FLAGS
//   overflow gate the long-flag / single-letter-short paths use. Two
//   lookups per letter is O(2 * cluster_len * SPEC_MAX) which is
//   still bounded above by 2 * 32 * 32 = 2048 comparisons per parse.
//
//   The cluster path deliberately does NOT fire the ENH-032
//   (--version) or ENH-014 (--help) auto-emit dispatch: a tool that
//   registers `-h`/`-v` short with those std ids AND embeds them in
//   a cluster is outside the mainstream `-vvv` idiom, and the
//   two-pass expansion loses the natural "first store, then check"
//   auto-emit shape. The single-letter short-flag path is unchanged
//   for that idiom; a tool that wants `-h` auto-help just types it
//   without clustering.
//
//   Scratch buffer sizing: cluster_scratch_buf is 64 bytes = 2 bytes
//   per letter × MAX_FLAGS (32). Since every cluster store increments
//   flag_count, and the overflow gate at flag_count == 32 fires
//   before any 33rd letter's scratch write, the 64-byte bound is
//   exactly right. parsed_args_reset zeroes flag_count so the next
//   parse starts writing at scratch[0]; the buffer's stale bytes
//   never leak into the current parse because flag_names[k] pointers
//   land inside the freshly-overwritten region.
//
// libpdx-argv.ENH-017 (collect-all-errors, Closes #27):
//   `parse_argv_ex(argv, argc, parse_flags)` is the ENH-017 primary
//   entry point; `parse_argv(argv, argc)` becomes a thin wrapper
//   that forwards `parse_flags = 0` (byte-for-byte identical
//   behaviour to the pre-ENH-017 shape). Bit 0 of `parse_flags`
//   (`ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS`) toggles multi-error accumulation:
//
//     - The FIRST error still lands in ParsedArgs::error_code /
//       error_arg_index and in error_ring[0], regardless of the bit.
//     - With the bit UNSET (default; every existing caller), the
//       parser returns after that first error exactly as before.
//     - With the bit SET, the parser records the error into
//       error_ring[error_ring_count++] and advances past the
//       offending argv slot instead of returning. Up to
//       MAX_COLLECTED_ERRORS (16) records accumulate before
//       subsequent errors are silently dropped from the ring;
//       ERR_FLAG_OVERFLOW / ERR_POS_OVERFLOW still stop the parse
//       (the storage arrays are full; continuing would trip the
//       same gate on every subsequent slot). The parser returns
//       the FIRST-error code on any exit path where the ring has
//       content — consumers observe the same "first error wins"
//       return-value semantics whether they read via the pre-
//       existing scalar slots or the new ring API.
//
//   The bit also enables inline INT-value validation: after every
//   store whose kind == FKIND_INT and value != null, the parser
//   calls Typed::parse_int_u64(value) and records ERR_BAD_INT (17)
//   into the ring on a decode failure. This validation is opt-in
//   with the bit: `parse_argv(argv, argc)` never calls the decoder
//   at parse time (pre-ENH-017 contract preserved — the consumer's
//   dispatch-site decoder still catches bad values), so no existing
//   caller sees a behavior change.
//
//   Implementation shape: parse_flags is spilled to the alignment
//   slot at `[rsp]` (the 8-byte pad the 6-push prologue already
//   reserves for stack alignment) and reloaded whenever a fail
//   site needs to gate on the bit. The alignment slot was
//   previously unused padding; ENH-017 repurposes it without
//   changing the prologue shape or the nested-call alignment
//   invariant. Every recoverable-fail label (parse_argv_unknown_flag,
//   parse_argv_missing_value, parse_argv_unknown_arg_form,
//   parse_argv_long_missing_name, parse_argv_cluster_with_arity,
//   parse_argv_bad_int) jumps to parse_argv_maybe_collect instead
//   of the direct fail path. The two overflow labels
//   (parse_argv_flag_overflow, parse_argv_pos_overflow) still fall
//   through to parse_argv_fail directly.
//
//   New klog tag: `pdxargv.collect-errors`.
//
// libpdx-argv.ENH-019 (STRING flag enum validation, Closes #29):
//   After the long-flag or single-letter short-flag store block
//   completes (before the ENH-017 FKIND_INT gate and before the
//   ENH-032 / ENH-014 auto-emit dispatches), the parser inspects
//   the two FlagSpec companion slots `last_lookup_allowed_ptr` /
//   `last_lookup_allowed_count` that the preceding `call lookup`
//   published. If the just-stored kind is FKIND_STR (1) AND the
//   value pointer is non-null AND the allowed_count is nonzero,
//   the parser walks the allowed-values array (an array of
//   NUL-terminated string pointers registered by
//   `FlagSpec::register_string_enum`) and byte-compares each
//   candidate against the captured value using the same inline
//   xor+mov_b+cmp strcmp shape lookup() itself uses. First byte-
//   equal match wins; no match after walking every candidate fails
//   the parse with `ERR_STRING_ENUM (18)` via `parse_argv_maybe_collect`
//   (recoverable — routes through collect-all recovery just like
//   ERR_BAD_INT does under ENH-017's bit).
//
//   The gate fires on BOTH `parse_argv` and `parse_argv_ex`
//   (regardless of the ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS bit): the allowed
//   set is a per-registration property that was explicitly opted
//   into at `register_string_enum` time, so the check is orthogonal
//   to the collect-mode opt-in. The empty-list default (every
//   non-register_string_enum path writes `spec_allowed_count = 0`)
//   silently disables the gate; existing consumers that call
//   `flag_spec_register(name, FKIND_STR, id)` see zero behaviour
//   change. The check is case-sensitive: `"AUTO"` never matches an
//   `"auto"` entry — the byte compare uses xor+mov_b loads only,
//   with no ASCII-case normalisation, matching the byte-for-byte
//   discipline of the rest of the parser.
//
//   The store itself is NOT rolled back: `flag_names[k] /
//   flag_values[k] / flag_ids[k] / flag_kinds[k]` all reflect the
//   (name, offending-value, id, STR) triple the parser observed —
//   same discipline as ERR_BAD_INT (ENH-017). Consumers walking
//   ParsedArgs after a parse that returned ERR_STRING_ENUM should
//   treat the offending FKIND_STR slot as diagnostic-only. The
//   cluster path (`parse_argv_short_clustered`) is BOOL-only by
//   ENH-013's admissibility rule, so no enum walk fires there.
//
//   New klog tag: `pdxargv.string-enum`.
//
// libpdx-argv.ENH-020 (git-shape subcommand dispatch, Closes #30):
//   A caller enables git-shape subcommand dispatch by installing a
//   subcommand table via FlagSpec::register_subcommands(table_ptr,
//   count) at bootstrap. Each SubSpec entry (SUBSPEC_STRIDE = 40
//   bytes) carries {name_ptr, id, flag_specs_ptr, flag_count,
//   help_ptr}; each entry in a SubSpec's flag_specs array
//   (SUBFLAGSPEC_STRIDE = 24 bytes) carries {name_ptr, kind, id}
//   mirroring FlagSpec's 3-arg register surface.
//
//   Dispatch phase (runs BEFORE the main argv walk, gated on
//   subcommand_table_ptr != 0):
//     1. Skip argv[0] as program name (r13 starts at 1 for every
//        table-registered parse; the pre-ENH-020 argv[0]-included
//        semantic is preserved for callers that never register a
//        sub table).
//     2. If argc < 2 → no dispatch; walk starts at r13 = 1 with the
//        top-level FlagSpec table intact.
//     3. If argv[1] starts with '-' OR is empty → treat as
//        top-level flag: no dispatch, top-level FlagSpec intact,
//        walk starts at r13 = 1. This is what makes
//        `git --version` still trigger the StdVocab --version
//        auto-emit (assuming the caller registered StdVocab at
//        top level via `register_all`).
//     4. Otherwise, walk the sub table byte-comparing (case-
//        sensitive) argv[1] against each SubSpec.name via the same
//        inline xor+mov_b+cmp strcmp shape FlagSpec::lookup uses.
//        First match wins.
//     5. On match: publish the matched SubSpec.id into
//        ParsedArgs::subcommand_id, then hoist SubSpec.flag_specs_ptr
//        + SubSpec.flag_count into callee-save r14/r15, call
//        flag_spec_reset (wipes the top-level table AND the sub
//        table pointer/count so no nested dispatch fires from
//        inside the sub's parse), then walk the flag_specs array
//        calling flag_spec_register(name, kind, id) per entry.
//        The parse loop then starts at r13 = 2 (both argv[0] and
//        argv[1] are consumed — argv[0] as program name, argv[1]
//        as the sub name).
//     6. On no match: rax = ERR_UNKNOWN_SUBCOMMAND (19), r13 = 1
//        (the offending argv slot), jump to parse_argv_fail. No
//        FlagSpec state is touched on this path — the reject
//        happens BEFORE the flag_spec_reset + sub install block,
//        so a caller inspecting FlagSpec after the fail sees the
//        top-level table intact (a subsequent flag_spec_reset
//        from the harness zeroes it as usual). This mirrors
//        ENH-013's cluster-fail "no partial commit" discipline.
//
//   StdVocab precedence (`--version` / `--help` at top level): a
//   flag-shaped argv[1] skips the sub table walk entirely — the
//   parser proceeds into the main loop with the top-level
//   FlagSpec intact, so `--version` / `--help` fire their auto-
//   emit or normal-store paths exactly as they do without a sub
//   table. This matches the git-shape convention where
//   `git --version` prints the top-level version but
//   `git commit --version` (if a tool registers --version inside
//   the commit sub's flag_specs) prints the sub's version.
//
//   The dispatch phase runs on parse_argv_ex only (parse_argv is
//   still the thin wrapper); the ENH-017 collect-all bit is
//   orthogonal: an unknown-subcommand fail is a hard-stop
//   (routed through parse_argv_fail, not parse_argv_maybe_collect),
//   because no ParsedArgs storage was ever attempted — the
//   accumulator semantic (advance past the offending slot and
//   keep parsing) has no coherent meaning when the flag spec
//   table itself was not installed.
//
//   Nested subcommands (`pkg install --dry-run …` where `install`
//   would itself carry sub-subs) are out of scope for ENH-020;
//   the flag_spec_reset on match wipes the sub table pointer so
//   the sub's parse never re-enters the dispatch phase. A
//   follow-on ENH would re-install a per-sub sub table before
//   the sub's parse; the semver-safe shape is a
//   SubSpec.sub_table_ptr / sub_table_count pair the parser reads
//   between the flag_spec_reset and the flag_spec_register loop.
//
//   New klog tag: `pdxargv.subcommand`.
//
// libpdx-argv.ENH-026 (malformed-argv-slot classification):
//   The classifier now rejects two argv shapes that fit neither the
//   flag nor the positional grammar with
//   ParsedArgs::ERR_UNKNOWN_ARG_FORM (3) and error_arg_index = the
//   offending argv index:
//     - empty string ""                      (byte0 == NUL)
//     - bare "-"                             (byte0 == '-', byte1 == NUL)
//   Bare "--" is UNCHANGED — it remains the M2-003 positional
//   sentinel and sets ddash_seen. Slots that appear after the
//   sentinel are always positional (via the ddash gate at loop head)
//   and never reach the malformed classifier, so an empty "" or bare
//   "-" AFTER `--` is stored as a positional as before. Pre-ENH-026
//   the two shapes above landed in pos_ptrs (bare "-" was documented
//   as "stdin convention" — tools that want the stdin idiom must now
//   register it themselves, e.g. as a tool-specific "stdin" flag, or
//   filter their argv before calling parse_argv).
//
// See design/architecture.md §4 (M1 state machine) and §9 (M2
// extensions). Upstream: design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §3.3 + §5.12
// (paideia-os).
//
// Cross-module symbols read/written (all resolved by unqualified name):
//   ParsedArgs:      flag_names, flag_values, flag_count, pos_ptrs,
//                    pos_count, error_code, error_arg_index, emit_schema,
//                    flag_ids, flag_kinds, ddash_seen, ddash_arg_index,
//                    subcommand_id (ENH-020, Closes #30)
//   FlagSpec::       lookup (called via qualified name),
//                    subcommand_table_ptr, subcommand_table_count
//                    (read by ENH-020 dispatch phase),
//                    flag_spec_reset, flag_spec_register (called by
//                    ENH-020 dispatch phase on match)
//   VersionBackend:: override_enabled, emit_default (ENH-032, Closes #25)
//   HelpBackend::    doc_backend_unavailable, emit_from_argspec
//                    (ENH-014, Closes #24)
//
// paideia-as compliance
// ---------------------
//   - Module Parser (PascalCase basename).
//   - No test mnemonic; every zero-check uses cmp reg, 0.
//   - Every cmp reg, imm ≤ 0x7FFFFFFF (max: 0xFF for FKIND_UNKNOWN,
//     and 32 for the overflow checks; single-byte ASCII for chars).
//   - r11 is scratch (used in every lea for cross-module .bss bases).
//   - Byte loads use xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [ptr] per #1248.
//   - SysV push/pop parity: 6 pushes (rbx, rbp, r12, r13, r14, r15)
//     + sub rsp, 8 = 7 slots on stack after the return address, so
//     rsp%16 = 0 at every nested call site. Epilogue mirrors exactly.

module Parser = structure {

  // -- ENH-013 cluster scratch buffers (Closes #23) -------------------
  // Parser-owned .bss buffers backing the short-flag cluster expansion
  // path. Both are written into during parse_argv and never read
  // outside it. See the ENH-013 preamble note above for the sizing
  // rationale (64-byte scratch = 2 bytes × MAX_FLAGS).
  //
  //   cluster_probe          — 2-byte NUL-terminated scratch string
  //                            (letter + NUL) handed to FlagSpec::lookup
  //                            during pass 1 (validation). Overwritten
  //                            once per cluster letter; never read by
  //                            consumers.
  //   cluster_scratch_buf    — the storage for expanded letter names.
  //                            Keyed by flag_count*2 during pass 2
  //                            (dispatch): the k-th store writes
  //                            scratch[flag_count*2] = letter and
  //                            scratch[flag_count*2 + 1] = NUL, then
  //                            publishes scratch+flag_count*2 into
  //                            flag_names[flag_count]. Pointers into
  //                            this buffer are live for the lifetime
  //                            of the ParsedArgs record (i.e. until
  //                            the next parsed_args_reset).
  pub let mut cluster_probe       : [u64; 1] = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut cluster_scratch_buf : [u64; 8] = uninit @align(8)

  // parse_argv_ex(argv, argc, parse_flags) → u64
  //   rdi = argv (u64* array of NUL-terminated arg pointers)
  //   rsi = argc (count of entries in argv)
  //   rdx = parse_flags (bit 0 = ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS; other bits
  //         reserved, must be 0 — currently ignored)
  //   rax = 0 (ERR_OK) on success, else one of the ERR_* codes in
  //         ParsedArgs. On error the offending arg index is also
  //         recorded to ParsedArgs::error_arg_index for the
  //         diagnostic renderer. In collect-all mode, if the parser
  //         reaches the end of argv after N > 0 recoverable errors,
  //         it returns the FIRST error's code — the same value the
  //         pre-ENH-017 shape would have returned had it stopped
  //         parsing at the first error.
  //
  // Non-leaf (calls FlagSpec::lookup once per flag; also calls
  // Typed::parse_int_u64 in collect-all mode for every FKIND_INT
  // flag with a value).
  //
  // Register plan (callee-save preserved via 6-push + 8-byte pad):
  //   rbx = argv base pointer
  //   rbp = temporary name ptr / other spill (preserved across
  //         FlagSpec::lookup calls)
  //   r12 = argc
  //   r13 = i (loop counter; may be incremented mid-iteration when
  //         a typed flag consumes argv[i+1])
  //   r14 = value ptr for the flag currently being stored
  //         (0 = boolean; else pointer into argv-owned memory)
  //   r15 = id from FlagSpec::lookup for the flag currently being
  //         stored (0 = unregistered)
  //
  // Stack local (ENH-017):
  //   [rsp + 0]  = parse_flags (u64; spilled to the alignment-pad
  //                slot the 6-push prologue reserves. The slot was
  //                previously unused padding; ENH-017 stores
  //                parse_flags there and reloads on each fail-path
  //                gate so a nested-call clobber never loses it.
  //                The prologue shape is unchanged so the rsp%16
  //                invariant for nested calls is preserved.)
  //
  // Scratch (caller-save; may be trashed across call lookup):
  //   rax = byte load / lookup return kind / return value
  //   rcx = walk cursor / storage slot index / parse_flags reload
  //   rdx = argv[i] pointer (reloaded from [rbx + r13*8] as needed)
  //   rdi = arg to FlagSpec::lookup
  //   rsi = unused
  //   r8, r9, r10 = ephemeral compute
  //   r11 = lea scratch for cross-module .bss bases
  pub let parse_argv_ex : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (argv: u64) (argc: u64) (parse_flags: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-argv.M2 + ENH-004 + ENH-013 + ENH-014 + ENH-017 + ENH-019 + ENH-020 + ENH-026 + ENH-032 parse_argv_ex entry point. ENH-020 (Closes #30): pre-loop subcommand dispatch phase gated on FlagSpec::subcommand_table_ptr != 0. When installed, argv[0] is skipped as program name (r13 starts at 1 by default), and if argv[1] is a plausible non-flag non-empty candidate the parser inline-strcmps it against each SubSpec.name in the caller-owned table (40-byte stride). First match wins → publish SubSpec.id into ParsedArgs::subcommand_id, hoist SubSpec.flag_specs_ptr / flag_count into callee-save r14/r15, call flag_spec_reset (wipes top-level FlagSpec AND the sub table pointer so no nested dispatch fires from the sub's own parse), then walk flag_specs (24-byte stride) calling flag_spec_register(name, kind, id) per entry; loop then starts at r13=2. Flag-shaped argv[1] (byte0 == '-') OR empty argv[1] OR missing argv[1] → no dispatch, top-level FlagSpec intact, loop starts at r13=1 (so StdVocab's --version / --help still fire from `git --version` / `git --help`). Non-flag argv[1] with no SubSpec match → ERR_UNKNOWN_SUBCOMMAND (19) via parse_argv_fail (hard-stop; no partial FlagSpec state touched — the reject happens BEFORE the flag_spec_reset in the match arm). The prologue's 6-push + sub rsp,8 keeps rsp%16 = 0 across the added flag_spec_reset and flag_spec_register nested calls in the match path (both callees preserve r13/r14/r15 since those are SysV callee-save; the callees themselves only clobber rax/rcx/r11 per their docstrings, so no stack spill of loop state is needed). --- Non-leaf: consults FlagSpec::lookup(name) once per parsed flag to decide arity (BOOL/UNKNOWN → no lookahead; typed → consume argv[i+1] or ERR_MISSING_VALUE). ENH-017 (Closes #27) generalises the pre-ENH-017 parse_argv into a three-argument primary — the third argument (parse_flags: u64) is spilled to [rsp+0] (the alignment-pad slot the 6-push prologue already reserves; ENH-017 repurposes that byte-for-byte-idle padding without changing the prologue shape) and reloaded on every recoverable-fail gate. Bit 0 (ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS): parser records each fail into ParsedArgs::error_ring_* and advances past the offending argv slot instead of returning; up to MAX_COLLECTED_ERRORS (16) records accumulate before subsequent fails are silently dropped from the ring. Also enables inline FKIND_INT value validation: after every store whose kind == 2 (FKIND_INT) and value != 0, calls Typed::parse_int_u64(value) and records ERR_BAD_INT (17) on ok == 0. Bit unset (parse_argv thin wrapper) preserves the pre-ENH-017 return-on-first-error behavior byte-for-byte, including that no inline INT validation is performed. First-error semantics: the FIRST error observed always writes error_code / error_arg_index and lands in error_ring[0]; the return value on any exit path with ring content is that first-error code (parse_argv_epilogue reads error_ring_codes[0] into rax before ret). ENH-004: immediately after every lookup, if the returned kind is FKIND_UNKNOWN and FlagSpec::strict_mode is nonzero, fails with ERR_UNKNOWN_FLAG (12) before the inline-value short-circuit, so a strict-mode-rejected flag never gets a chance to store via its `=`/`:` inline value either; strict_mode==0 (the default FlagSpec::reset() restores) reproduces byte-identical pre-ENH-004 behaviour. Also handles the M2-003 `--` sentinel (records ddash_seen + ddash_arg_index; every subsequent argv[j] joins pos_ptrs regardless of leading byte) and the `:` value separator (used by `--no-cap:KIND_TTY` per I3). Prologue pushes rbx/rbp/r12/r13/r14/r15 (6 callee-save preserved for loop state + call-crossing spills) and adds sub rsp, 8 to bring rsp%16 to 0 for the nested call. Every store into ParsedArgs writes flag_ids and flag_kinds alongside the raw name/value pointers so consumers can dispatch via ParsedArgs::find_flag_by_id. Well-known --pdx-schema compare preserved from M1 (sets emit_schema=1 independently of FlagSpec registration). ENH-032 (Closes #25): after every long-flag or short-flag store, the just-stored id is checked against STD_ID_VERSION (2); on a match with VersionBackend::override_enabled == 0, the parser calls VersionBackend::emit_default and returns ERR_VERSION_EMITTED (13). ENH-014 (Closes #24): matching id 1 with HelpBackend::doc_backend_unavailable != 0 calls emit_from_argspec and returns ERR_HELP_EMITTED (15). These two auto-emit paths deliberately terminate the parse (they are success signals for the tool's exit-immediately behavior); they are NOT routed through the collect-all recovery machinery even when the bit is set. ENH-013 (Closes #23): the parse_argv_short_clustered arm is no longer an unconditional ERR_CLUSTERED_SHORT reject — it two-passes the cluster (validation via cluster_probe, dispatch via cluster_scratch_buf indexed by flag_count*2) and either expands `-abc` into three flag_count++ stores or fails with ERR_CLUSTER_WITH_ARITY (16) at the first value-consuming kind. ENH-019 (Closes #29): after every long-flag or short-flag store whose kind == FKIND_STR (1) AND value != null AND FlagSpec::last_lookup_allowed_count (published by the preceding call lookup, same shape as last_lookup_sep_required) is nonzero, the parser walks the caller-registered allowed-values array (an array of NUL-terminated string pointers, byte-compared case-sensitive via the same inline xor+mov_b+cmp shape lookup itself uses) and fails ERR_STRING_ENUM (18) on no match via parse_argv_string_enum → parse_argv_maybe_collect. The gate is on BOTH parse_argv and parse_argv_ex (independent of the ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS bit) because the allowed set is a per-registration property opted into at register_string_enum time; the empty-set default (count == 0) skips the walk so every non-string-enum FKIND_STR registration preserves pre-ENH-019 behaviour. The cluster path is BOOL-only per ENH-013's admissibility rule, so no enum walk fires there. The same 6-push + sub rsp,8 prologue keeps rsp%16 aligned for every added nested call (lookup/parse_int_u64/emit_default/emit_from_argspec).",
      block: {
        // ---- prologue (6 pushes + 8-byte pad → rsp%16 = 0) --------------
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 8;

        // ENH-017: spill parse_flags into the alignment slot at [rsp+0].
        // The slot was previously unused padding — reused here without
        // changing the prologue shape (rsp%16 stays 0 for nested calls).
        // Every fail-path gate below reloads via `mov rcx, [rsp]` and
        // tests bit 0 (ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS).
        mov [rsp + 0], rdx;

        mov rbx, rdi;                       // rbx = argv base
        mov r12, rsi;                       // r12 = argc
        xor r13, r13;                       // r13 = i = 0 (default: no sub dispatch)

        // -- ENH-020 (Closes #30) subcommand dispatch phase ------------
        // Gated on FlagSpec::subcommand_table_ptr != 0. See the module-
        // level preamble ENH-020 note for the full state-machine. In
        // brief:
        //   - Pointer OR count == 0                → no dispatch, r13=0
        //   - argc < 2                             → no dispatch, r13=1
        //   - argv[1][0] == '-' OR argv[1][0] == 0 → no dispatch, r13=1
        //   - argv[1] matches a SubSpec.name       → install sub's
        //                                            flag_specs, r13=2
        //   - argv[1] present but no match         → ERR_UNKNOWN_SUBCOMMAND
        //                                            via parse_argv_fail
        //
        // Register plan (all callee-save; leaf-safe across nested calls):
        //   r13 = final loop start index (0 / 1 / 2 by dispatch outcome;
        //         also reused as the scan index inside the dispatch phase
        //         itself since the parse loop has not yet started)
        //   r14 = subcommand_table_ptr, then sub's flag_specs_ptr
        //   r15 = subcommand_table_count, then sub's flag_count
        //   rax, rcx, r8, r9, r11 = scratch (all caller-save)
        //
        // rdx holds argv[1] throughout the sub-name walk; every strcmp
        // iteration takes a fresh copy into rsi so the walk cursor never
        // clobbers the base pointer. flag_spec_reset / flag_spec_register
        // clobber only rax/rcx/r11 (per their docstrings), so r13/r14/r15
        // survive both nested calls without a stack spill.
        lea r11, [rip + subcommand_table_ptr];
        mov rax, [r11];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_loop_head;            // no table → r13 stays 0

        // Table registered. Skip argv[0] as program name by default.
        mov r13, 1;

        lea r11, [rip + subcommand_table_count];
        mov r15, [r11];
        cmp r15, 0;
        je parse_argv_loop_head;            // empty table → no dispatch

        cmp r12, 2;
        jl parse_argv_loop_head;            // no argv[1] → no dispatch

        mov rdx, [rbx + 8];                 // rdx = argv[1]
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdx];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_loop_head;            // empty argv[1] → no dispatch
        cmp rax, 0x2D;                      // '-'
        je parse_argv_loop_head;            // flag-shaped argv[1] → no
                                            // dispatch (StdVocab handles
                                            // --version/--help at top level)

        // argv[1] is a plausible sub-name candidate. Walk the sub table.
        lea r11, [rip + subcommand_table_ptr];
        mov r14, [r11];                     // r14 = table_ptr
        mov r13, 0;                         // r13 = scan_idx (temporary; overwritten below)

      parse_argv_sub_scan_head:
        cmp r13, r15;
        jge parse_argv_sub_unknown;

        // entry_ptr = r14 + r13 * 40. Use shl+add (no 2-op imul; the
        // shift form stays inside the R49 encoder subset).
        mov r8, r13;
        shl r8, 5;                          // r13 * 32
        mov r9, r13;
        shl r9, 3;                          // r13 * 8
        add r8, r9;                         // r13 * 40
        add r8, r14;                        // &sub_table[r13]

        mov rdi, [r8 + 0];                  // sub.name (SUBSPEC_OFF_NAME = 0)
        mov rsi, rdx;                       // fresh argv[1] cursor

      parse_argv_sub_strcmp_head:
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rdi];
        xor r9, r9;   mov_b r9, [rsi];
        cmp rax, r9;
        jne parse_argv_sub_scan_next;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_sub_match;
        add rdi, 1;
        add rsi, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_sub_strcmp_head;

      parse_argv_sub_scan_next:
        add r13, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_sub_scan_head;

      parse_argv_sub_match:
        // r8 = matched SubSpec entry pointer. Publish subcommand_id,
        // hoist flag_specs_ptr / flag_count into r14/r15 (callee-save
        // → survive both nested calls), then reset FlagSpec and
        // register each SubFlagSpec entry.
        mov rax, [r8 + 8];                  // SUBSPEC_OFF_ID
        lea r11, [rip + subcommand_id];
        mov [r11], rax;

        mov r14, [r8 + 16];                 // SUBSPEC_OFF_FLAG_SPECS
        mov r15, [r8 + 24];                 // SUBSPEC_OFF_FLAG_COUNT

        call flag_spec_reset;               // wipes top-level table AND
                                            // the sub table pointer/count
                                            // (no nested dispatch fires
                                            // from the sub's own parse)

        xor r13, r13;                       // r13 = flag_specs walk idx

      parse_argv_sub_reg_head:
        cmp r13, r15;
        jge parse_argv_sub_reg_done;

        // entry_ptr = r14 + r13 * 24 (SUBFLAGSPEC_STRIDE).
        mov r8, r13;
        shl r8, 4;                          // r13 * 16
        mov r9, r13;
        shl r9, 3;                          // r13 * 8
        add r8, r9;                         // r13 * 24
        add r8, r14;                        // &flag_specs[r13]

        mov rdi, [r8 + 0];                  // SUBFLAGSPEC_OFF_NAME
        mov rsi, [r8 + 8];                  // SUBFLAGSPEC_OFF_KIND
        mov rdx, [r8 + 16];                 // SUBFLAGSPEC_OFF_ID
        call flag_spec_register;            // preserves r13/r14/r15
                                            // (uses only rax/rcx/r11 +
                                            // its own push rbx for align)

        add r13, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_sub_reg_head;

      parse_argv_sub_reg_done:
        // Both argv[0] (program name) and argv[1] (sub name) consumed.
        mov r13, 2;
        jmp parse_argv_loop_head;

      parse_argv_sub_unknown:
        // argv[1] is a plausible candidate but no SubSpec.name matched.
        // Route through the hard-fail path — ENH-017 collect-all mode
        // is inapplicable (no ParsedArgs storage attempted; no way to
        // advance-past coherently), and no FlagSpec state was touched
        // (this label runs BEFORE the flag_spec_reset in the match
        // path), so a caller inspecting FlagSpec after the fail sees
        // the top-level table intact.
        mov rax, 19;                        // ERR_UNKNOWN_SUBCOMMAND
        mov r13, 1;                         // argv_index = 1
        jmp parse_argv_fail;

      parse_argv_loop_head:
        cmp r13, r12;
        jge parse_argv_done_ok;

        mov rdx, [rbx + r13*8];             // rdx = argv[i]

        // -- ddash sentinel gate: if `--` was seen, every arg is positional
        lea r11, [rip + ddash_seen];
        mov rax, [r11];
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne parse_argv_positional;

        // -- Classify by argv[i][0]
        //    ENH-026: an empty argv slot ("") is malformed — it fits
        //    neither the flag grammar (no leading '-') nor the
        //    positional grammar (must be a non-empty bare string).
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdx];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_unknown_arg_form;     // ENH-026: empty string
        cmp rax, 0x2D;                      // '-'
        je parse_argv_maybe_flag;
        jmp parse_argv_positional;

      parse_argv_maybe_flag:
        // Byte1 check: 0x2D = another '-' (long or ddash), NUL = bare '-'
        //    ENH-026: a bare "-" is malformed — starts with '-' but
        //    has no name byte after it, so it is neither a valid short
        //    nor a valid long flag; and unlike ordinary positionals it
        //    still starts with '-' so it is not a valid positional
        //    either. Tools that want the historical stdin-'-' idiom
        //    must filter their argv before calling parse_argv, or
        //    register a tool-specific short-name for stdin.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdx + 1];
        cmp rax, 0x2D;
        je parse_argv_maybe_long_or_ddash;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_unknown_arg_form;     // ENH-026: bare "-"
        jmp parse_argv_short_flag;

      parse_argv_maybe_long_or_ddash:
        // Byte2 check.
        //   NUL → bare "--" (M2-003 sentinel: set ddash_seen + advance)
        //   '=' or ':' → "--=..." / "--:..." — LONG_MISSING_NAME
        //   else → long-flag body
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdx + 2];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_set_ddash;
        cmp rax, 0x3D;
        je parse_argv_long_missing_name;
        cmp rax, 0x3A;
        je parse_argv_long_missing_name;
        jmp parse_argv_long_flag_body;

      parse_argv_set_ddash:
        // Record the sentinel and skip storing it. Every subsequent
        // argv[j] takes the ddash gate at loop head.
        mov rax, 1;
        lea r11, [rip + ddash_seen];
        mov [r11], rax;
        mov rax, r13;
        lea r11, [rip + ddash_arg_index];
        mov [r11], rax;
        jmp parse_argv_advance_loop;

      parse_argv_long_flag_body:
        // rdx = arg ptr, valid. Preserve the name pointer (rdx + 2)
        // in rbp so it survives the FlagSpec::lookup call. Walk from
        // rbp looking for '=', ':', or NUL.
        mov rbp, rdx;
        add rbp, 2;                         // rbp = name ptr

        mov rcx, rbp;                       // walk cursor
        xor r14, r14;                       // value ptr = 0

      parse_argv_walk_name:
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rcx];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_name_no_eq;
        cmp rax, 0x3D;                      // '='
        je parse_argv_name_has_sep;
        cmp rax, 0x3A;                      // ':'
        je parse_argv_name_has_sep;
        add rcx, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_walk_name;

      parse_argv_name_has_sep:
        // In-place null-terminate at the separator; value = cursor + 1.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b [rcx], rax;
        mov r14, rcx;
        add r14, 1;
        // fallthrough to lookup

      parse_argv_name_no_eq:
        // Either r14 = 0 (no inline value; will consult lookup for
        // arity) or r14 = value ptr (from = or : form).
        mov rdi, rbp;                       // rdi = name ptr
        call lookup;              // rax=kind, rdx=id
        mov r15, rdx;                       // r15 = id (preserved for store)
        // rax = kind (preserved through the arity checks below since
        // those touch r14/r10/r12/r13/r14 not rax)

        // -- ENH-004 strict mode: reject unregistered flags --------------
        // Checked before the inline-value short-circuit below so
        // `--nosuchflag=foo` is rejected under strict mode exactly
        // like `--nosuchflag` is.
        cmp rax, 0xFF;                      // FKIND_UNKNOWN
        jne parse_argv_long_not_unknown;
        lea r11, [rip + strict_mode];
        mov r10, [r11];
        cmp r10, 0;
        je parse_argv_long_not_unknown;
        jmp parse_argv_unknown_flag;
      parse_argv_long_not_unknown:

        cmp r14, 0;
        jne parse_argv_long_do_store;       // value already set

        cmp rax, 0;                         // FKIND_BOOL
        je parse_argv_long_do_store;
        cmp rax, 0xFF;                      // FKIND_UNKNOWN
        je parse_argv_long_do_store;

        // -- ENH-010: separator-required flags never accept lookahead ----
        // last_lookup_sep_required was published by the `call lookup`
        // above; a nonzero value means this flag's spelling has no
        // lookahead form at all (e.g. --color=<mode>) — fail here
        // rather than swallowing argv[i+1] as its value.
        lea r11, [rip + last_lookup_sep_required];
        mov r10, [r11];
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne parse_argv_missing_value;

        // Typed flag, no inline value → consume argv[i+1] as value.
        mov r10, r13;
        add r10, 1;
        cmp r10, r12;
        jge parse_argv_missing_value;

        mov r14, [rbx + r10*8];             // r14 = argv[i+1]
        add r13, 1;                         // consume argv[i+1]

      parse_argv_long_do_store:
        // Store into ParsedArgs.
        //   rbp = name ptr, r14 = value ptr (0 for boolean),
        //   r15 = id, rax = kind (still valid from the lookup return —
        //   the paths above deliberately avoid clobbering rax).
        lea r11, [rip + flag_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];
        cmp rcx, 32;                        // MAX_FLAGS
        jge parse_argv_flag_overflow;

        lea r11, [rip + flag_names];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], rbp;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_values];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], r14;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_ids];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], r15;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_kinds];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], rax;

        add rcx, 1;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_count];
        mov [r11], rcx;

        // -- ENH-019: FKIND_STR enum validation (Closes #29) ----------
        // If the just-stored flag is FKIND_STR (1) AND its value is
        // non-null AND FlagSpec::last_lookup_allowed_count (published
        // by the preceding call lookup) is nonzero, byte-compare the
        // captured value against every entry in the allowed set. No
        // match → ERR_STRING_ENUM (18) via parse_argv_string_enum →
        // parse_argv_maybe_collect. Case-sensitive; empty allowed
        // set (count == 0, the plain-registration sentinel) skips
        // the walk entirely, so a plain flag_spec_register(name,
        // FKIND_STR, id) preserves pre-ENH-019 behaviour byte-for-
        // byte. rax still holds the kind from the lookup return
        // (paths above deliberately avoid clobbering rax); r14
        // still holds the value pointer.
        //
        // Placed BEFORE the ENH-017 FKIND_INT gate for a defensive
        // reason: on the STR path we clobber rax through the strcmp
        // walk (rax=0 on the match-out branch), and the subsequent
        // FKIND_INT gate's `cmp rax, 2` correctly sees not-INT and
        // skips. On the not-STR fallthrough rax is preserved so the
        // INT gate reads the kind unaltered. STR and INT are the
        // only two per-store post-processing gates today, and they
        // are mutually exclusive at the kind level, so their
        // relative order carries no observable behaviour beyond
        // this register-preservation contract.
        //
        // The gate fires under BOTH parse_argv and parse_argv_ex
        // (independent of the ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS bit): the
        // allowed set is a per-registration property opted into at
        // register_string_enum time, so its check is orthogonal to
        // ENH-017's collect opt-in. This differs from FKIND_INT
        // validation immediately below, which is gated on the bit
        // because parse_int_u64 has always been the consumer's own
        // dispatch-site responsibility and running it inline under
        // plain parse_argv would silently change behaviour for
        // every pre-ENH-017 INT-flag consumer.
        //
        // Register plan (all caller-save; no nested call, so no
        // stack-alignment discipline required):
        //   rax = kind (preserved from lookup on entry; used as
        //         byte-load scratch during the walk)
        //   rcx = allowed_count (loop upper bound)
        //   rdx = byte-load scratch (value cursor byte)
        //   r8  = allowed_ptr base (hoisted once)
        //   r9  = i (allowed[] index)
        //   rdi = allowed[i] walk cursor (candidate name byte)
        //   rsi = r14 copy (value walk cursor)
        //   r11 = lea scratch (companion .bss loads)
        // Preserved throughout: r13 (argv index), r14 (value ptr),
        // r15 (id), rbp (name ptr), rbx (argv base), r12 (argc).
        cmp rax, 1;                         // FKIND_STR
        jne parse_argv_long_skip_str_enum;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je parse_argv_long_skip_str_enum;
        lea r11, [rip + last_lookup_allowed_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je parse_argv_long_skip_str_enum;
        lea r11, [rip + last_lookup_allowed_ptr];
        mov r8, [r11];
        xor r9, r9;                         // r9 = i = 0

      parse_argv_long_str_enum_probe:
        cmp r9, rcx;
        jge parse_argv_string_enum;         // walked all → no match
        mov rdi, [r8 + r9*8];               // rdi = allowed[i]
        mov rsi, r14;                       // rsi = value walk cursor

      parse_argv_long_str_enum_cmp:
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rdi];
        xor rdx, rdx; mov_b rdx, [rsi];
        cmp rax, rdx;
        jne parse_argv_long_str_enum_next;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_long_skip_str_enum;   // both NUL → match
        add rdi, 1;
        add rsi, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_long_str_enum_cmp;

      parse_argv_long_str_enum_next:
        add r9, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_long_str_enum_probe;

      parse_argv_long_skip_str_enum:

        // -- ENH-017: FKIND_INT value validation under collect-all --
        // Fires only when parse_flags bit 0 (ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS)
        // is set AND the just-stored flag has kind == FKIND_INT (2)
        // AND its value pointer is non-null. rax still holds the kind
        // from the lookup return (the store paths above deliberately
        // avoid clobbering rax); r14 still holds the value pointer.
        // Calls Typed::parse_int_u64 to validate; on ok == 0, jumps
        // to parse_argv_bad_int which routes through
        // parse_argv_maybe_collect (records ERR_BAD_INT + advances
        // OR falls to epilogue when the bit is unset — but the bit
        // guard here already ensures the bit is set at this call
        // site, so the maybe_collect path always takes the advance
        // branch on this route). Placed BEFORE the ENH-032/ENH-014
        // auto-emit checks and the --pdx-schema inline compare
        // because those clobber rax via byte loads and would lose
        // the kind we need for the FKIND_INT test. INT flags cannot
        // legitimately be --version/--help/--pdx-schema (id 2 / 1 /
        // schema-word; all registered as FKIND_BOOL or STD_ID_HELP-
        // scoped), so this ordering has no observable effect on
        // the auto-emit dispatch.
        //
        // ENH-019 sequencing note: the STRING-enum walk above
        // clobbers rax to 0 on its match-out branch, which correctly
        // skips this INT gate (cmp rax, 2 → jne). On the not-STR
        // fallthrough (parse_argv_long_skip_str_enum reached
        // without entering the walk) rax is preserved intact.
        mov rcx, [rsp + 0];                 // parse_flags
        and rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je parse_argv_long_skip_int_validate;
        cmp rax, 2;                         // FKIND_INT
        jne parse_argv_long_skip_int_validate;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je parse_argv_long_skip_int_validate;
        mov rdi, r14;                       // rdi = value ptr
        call parse_int_u64;                 // rax = ok, rdx = val
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_bad_int;              // ok == 0 → record + advance
      parse_argv_long_skip_int_validate:

        // -- ENH-032: --version auto-emit dispatch (Closes #25) --------
        // If the just-stored flag is StdVocab's --version (id 2) and
        // the tool has not opted out via
        // VersionBackend::set_override(1), call the library-owned
        // emitter and return ERR_VERSION_EMITTED via parse_argv_fail.
        // r15 still holds the id from the store above; r10/r11/rax are
        // caller-save and safe to clobber here. The nested call to
        // emit_default is aligned by parse_argv's own 6-push +
        // sub rsp,8 prologue (rsp%16 = 0 at every nested call site);
        // control never returns to parse_argv_advance_loop on the
        // auto-emit path — the tool exits after this return.
        cmp r15, 2;                         // STD_ID_VERSION
        jne parse_argv_long_not_version;
        lea r11, [rip + override_enabled];
        mov r10, [r11];
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne parse_argv_long_not_version;
        call emit_default;
        mov rax, 13;                        // ERR_VERSION_EMITTED
        jmp parse_argv_fail;
      parse_argv_long_not_version:

        // -- ENH-014: --help auto-emit dispatch (Closes #24) ------------
        // If the just-stored flag is StdVocab's --help (id 1) and
        // the tool has signalled `doc` back-end unavailability via
        // HelpBackend::set_doc_unavailable(1), call the library-owned
        // emitter and return ERR_HELP_EMITTED via parse_argv_fail.
        // The gate is opt-IN (default doc_backend_unavailable == 0
        // preserves the M3-002 dispatch: --help is stored as an
        // ordinary flag for the tool's own doc-forwarding code), so a
        // tool that has doc statically linked sees zero behavior
        // change on --help. r15 still holds the id from the store
        // above; r10/r11/rax are caller-save and safe to clobber. The
        // nested call to emit_from_argspec is aligned by parse_argv's
        // own 6-push + sub rsp,8 prologue (rsp%16 = 0 at every nested
        // call site); control never returns to parse_argv_advance_loop
        // on the auto-emit path — the tool exits after this return.
        cmp r15, 1;                         // STD_ID_HELP
        jne parse_argv_long_not_help;
        lea r11, [rip + doc_backend_unavailable];
        mov r10, [r11];
        cmp r10, 0;
        je parse_argv_long_not_help;
        call emit_from_argspec;
        mov rax, 15;                        // ERR_HELP_EMITTED
        jmp parse_argv_fail;
      parse_argv_long_not_help:

        // -- Well-known --pdx-schema inline compare (M1 back-compat) --
        // If name (rbp) equals "pdx-schema\0" byte-for-byte, set
        // emit_schema=1. Any mismatch falls through to advance.
        // This runs in addition to (not instead of) the FlagSpec-based
        // storage above — a consumer that registers --pdx-schema still
        // sees flag_ids[k] populated AND emit_schema toggled.
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 0];  cmp rax, 0x70; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // 'p'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 1];  cmp rax, 0x64; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // 'd'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 2];  cmp rax, 0x78; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // 'x'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 3];  cmp rax, 0x2D; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // '-'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 4];  cmp rax, 0x73; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // 's'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 5];  cmp rax, 0x63; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // 'c'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 6];  cmp rax, 0x68; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // 'h'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 7];  cmp rax, 0x65; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // 'e'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 8];  cmp rax, 0x6D; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // 'm'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 9];  cmp rax, 0x61; jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // 'a'
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rbp + 10]; cmp rax, 0;    jne parse_argv_advance_loop;  // NUL

        mov rax, 1;
        lea r11, [rip + emit_schema];
        mov [r11], rax;
        jmp parse_argv_advance_loop;

      parse_argv_short_flag:
        // rdx = arg ptr; rdx[0]='-'; rdx[1] is neither '-' nor NUL.
        // Check byte2: NUL → single-letter short flag; else clustered.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdx + 2];
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne parse_argv_short_clustered;

        // Single-letter short flag. Name = rdx + 1 (a 1-char + NUL
        // string that is a valid C-style string for FlagSpec::lookup).
        mov rbp, rdx;
        add rbp, 1;                         // rbp = name ptr
        xor r14, r14;                       // value = 0

        mov rdi, rbp;
        call lookup;              // rax=kind, rdx=id
        mov r15, rdx;

        // -- ENH-004 strict mode: reject unregistered flags --------------
        cmp rax, 0xFF;                      // FKIND_UNKNOWN
        jne parse_argv_short_not_unknown;
        lea r11, [rip + strict_mode];
        mov r10, [r11];
        cmp r10, 0;
        je parse_argv_short_not_unknown;
        jmp parse_argv_unknown_flag;
      parse_argv_short_not_unknown:

        cmp rax, 0;                         // FKIND_BOOL
        je parse_argv_short_do_store;
        cmp rax, 0xFF;                      // FKIND_UNKNOWN
        je parse_argv_short_do_store;

        // -- ENH-010: separator-required flags never accept lookahead ----
        // Note: the short-flag grammar has no `=`/`:` inline-value form
        // at all, so a separator-required flag registered under a
        // single-letter name can never be satisfied via this path —
        // it always fails here. That is a corollary of the policy,
        // not a special case: I3's two separator-required flags
        // (--color, --no-cap) are long-only and StdVocab never
        // registers short aliases for them (see std_vocab.pdx).
        lea r11, [rip + last_lookup_sep_required];
        mov r10, [r11];
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne parse_argv_missing_value;

        // Typed short flag → consume argv[i+1] as value.
        mov r10, r13;
        add r10, 1;
        cmp r10, r12;
        jge parse_argv_missing_value;

        mov r14, [rbx + r10*8];
        add r13, 1;

      parse_argv_short_do_store:
        lea r11, [rip + flag_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];
        cmp rcx, 32;
        jge parse_argv_flag_overflow;

        lea r11, [rip + flag_names];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], rbp;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_values];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], r14;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_ids];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], r15;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_kinds];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], rax;

        add rcx, 1;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_count];
        mov [r11], rcx;

        // -- ENH-019: FKIND_STR enum validation (Closes #29) ----------
        // Mirror of the long-flag block above (same register plan,
        // same fail routing to parse_argv_string_enum). Only the
        // labels differ (parse_argv_short_* prefix) so the two
        // paths can coexist without label collisions. See the
        // long-flag block for the full rationale.
        cmp rax, 1;                         // FKIND_STR
        jne parse_argv_short_skip_str_enum;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je parse_argv_short_skip_str_enum;
        lea r11, [rip + last_lookup_allowed_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je parse_argv_short_skip_str_enum;
        lea r11, [rip + last_lookup_allowed_ptr];
        mov r8, [r11];
        xor r9, r9;                         // r9 = i = 0

      parse_argv_short_str_enum_probe:
        cmp r9, rcx;
        jge parse_argv_string_enum;         // walked all → no match
        mov rdi, [r8 + r9*8];               // rdi = allowed[i]
        mov rsi, r14;                       // rsi = value walk cursor

      parse_argv_short_str_enum_cmp:
        xor rax, rax; mov_b rax, [rdi];
        xor rdx, rdx; mov_b rdx, [rsi];
        cmp rax, rdx;
        jne parse_argv_short_str_enum_next;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_short_skip_str_enum;  // both NUL → match
        add rdi, 1;
        add rsi, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_short_str_enum_cmp;

      parse_argv_short_str_enum_next:
        add r9, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_short_str_enum_probe;

      parse_argv_short_skip_str_enum:

        // -- ENH-017: FKIND_INT value validation under collect-all --
        // Mirror of the long-flag gate above (same rationale; see
        // parse_argv_long_skip_int_validate). Fires only when
        // parse_flags bit 0 is set AND kind == FKIND_INT AND value
        // pointer is non-null. rax and r14 preserved into the gate
        // from the store block above.
        mov rcx, [rsp + 0];                 // parse_flags
        and rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je parse_argv_short_skip_int_validate;
        cmp rax, 2;                         // FKIND_INT
        jne parse_argv_short_skip_int_validate;
        cmp r14, 0;
        je parse_argv_short_skip_int_validate;
        mov rdi, r14;
        call parse_int_u64;                 // rax = ok, rdx = val
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_bad_int;
      parse_argv_short_skip_int_validate:

        // -- ENH-032: --version auto-emit dispatch (Closes #25) --------
        // Same check as the long-flag store path. StdVocab does not
        // register a short alias for --version — a `-v` would collide
        // with grep/tail's invert-match convention — so this path
        // only fires for a tool that has explicitly registered a
        // single-letter short flag with id == STD_ID_VERSION (2).
        // Consumers wanting a `-V` short alias for the library-owned
        // auto-emit get it by calling flag_spec_register(&NAME_V,
        // FKIND_BOOL, 2) after register_all.
        cmp r15, 2;                         // STD_ID_VERSION
        jne parse_argv_short_not_version;
        lea r11, [rip + override_enabled];
        mov r10, [r11];
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne parse_argv_short_not_version;
        call emit_default;
        mov rax, 13;                        // ERR_VERSION_EMITTED
        jmp parse_argv_fail;
      parse_argv_short_not_version:

        // -- ENH-014: --help auto-emit dispatch (Closes #24) ------------
        // Mirror of the long-flag check above. StdVocab does not
        // register a short alias for --help — a `-h` would collide
        // with head/hexdump conventions — so this path only fires
        // for a tool that has explicitly registered a single-letter
        // short flag with id == STD_ID_HELP (1). Consumers wanting
        // a `-?` or `-h` alias for the library-owned auto-table get
        // it by calling flag_spec_register(&NAME_H, FKIND_BOOL, 1)
        // after register_all.
        cmp r15, 1;                         // STD_ID_HELP
        jne parse_argv_short_not_help;
        lea r11, [rip + doc_backend_unavailable];
        mov r10, [r11];
        cmp r10, 0;
        je parse_argv_short_not_help;
        call emit_from_argspec;
        mov rax, 15;                        // ERR_HELP_EMITTED
        jmp parse_argv_fail;
      parse_argv_short_not_help:

        jmp parse_argv_advance_loop;

      parse_argv_short_clustered:
        // ENH-013 (Closes #23): try to expand the cluster instead of
        // rejecting outright. rdx = arg ptr; rdx[0]='-'; rdx[1..N] are
        // the cluster letters; rdx[N+1]=NUL with N >= 2. Two-pass:
        // pass 1 validates every letter is admissible (BOOL, or
        // permissive-mode UNKNOWN → treated as BOOL); pass 2 walks
        // again and dispatches each letter as its own short-flag
        // store. See the ENH-013 module-level preamble for the full
        // rationale and scratch-buffer sizing.

        // -- Pass 1: validation --
        // rbp = validation cursor; starts at first letter (rdx + 1).
        mov rbp, rdx;
        add rbp, 1;

      parse_argv_cluster_val_head:
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rbp];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_cluster_val_done;

        // Build cluster_probe = <letter>\0 and hand to FlagSpec::lookup.
        lea r11, [rip + cluster_probe];
        mov_b [r11], rax;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b [r11 + 1], rax;

        mov rdi, r11;
        call lookup;                        // rax=kind, rdx=id

        cmp rax, 0xFF;                      // FKIND_UNKNOWN?
        jne parse_argv_cluster_val_check_kind;
        // Unknown letter. Under ENH-004 strict mode this is a hard
        // reject; permissive mode treats it as boolean (admissible).
        lea r11, [rip + strict_mode];
        mov r10, [r11];
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne parse_argv_unknown_flag;
        jmp parse_argv_cluster_val_advance;

      parse_argv_cluster_val_check_kind:
        // Only FKIND_BOOL (0) is admissible in a cluster. Every other
        // kind (STR/INT/TIMESPAN/SIZE/ENUM) would consume a value —
        // fail the whole cluster with ERR_CLUSTER_WITH_ARITY before
        // any letter is dispatched (no partial storage).
        cmp rax, 0;                         // FKIND_BOOL
        jne parse_argv_cluster_with_arity;

      parse_argv_cluster_val_advance:
        add rbp, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_cluster_val_head;

      parse_argv_cluster_val_done:
        // -- Pass 2: dispatch --
        // Reload rdx = argv[i] (clobbered across the pass-1 lookups).
        // rbp resets to the first letter.
        mov rdx, [rbx + r13*8];
        mov rbp, rdx;
        add rbp, 1;

      parse_argv_cluster_disp_head:
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rbp];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je parse_argv_cluster_disp_done;

        // -- MAX_FLAGS overflow gate (BEFORE any scratch write) ------
        // Reuses the same parse_argv_flag_overflow fail path the long
        // and single-short store paths use. Because flag_count is the
        // scratch index too, the 64-byte cluster_scratch_buf can
        // never be written past its last legal 2-byte pair: on the
        // 33rd cluster letter (flag_count == 32) this gate fires
        // first and no scratch write happens.
        lea r11, [rip + flag_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];
        cmp rcx, 32;                        // MAX_FLAGS
        jge parse_argv_flag_overflow;

        // Compute &cluster_scratch_buf[rcx * 2]. Use `add r9, r9` for
        // the *2 doubling — `shl r9, 1` also works but the add form
        // keeps the code inside the exact instruction set every other
        // parser path uses (no shifts in parse_argv currently).
        mov r9, rcx;
        add r9, r9;
        lea r11, [rip + cluster_scratch_buf];
        add r11, r9;

        // Write letter+NUL at scratch[rcx*2 .. rcx*2 + 2).
        mov_b [r11], rax;                   // letter (low byte of rax)
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b [r11 + 1], rax;               // NUL terminator

        // Preserve the name pointer in r14 (callee-save; survives the
        // lookup call) so we can store it into flag_names[rcx] after
        // the call returns. r14 is otherwise the value-ptr staging
        // register — safe to repurpose here because cluster stores
        // are BOOL-only (value = 0 always).
        mov r14, r11;

        mov rdi, r14;                       // rdi = name ptr
        call lookup;                        // rax=kind, rdx=id
        mov r15, rdx;                       // r15 = id

        // Reload rcx = flag_count (unchanged across lookup — leaf,
        // does not touch flag_count — but rcx itself was clobbered).
        lea r11, [rip + flag_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];

        lea r11, [rip + flag_names];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], r14;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_values];
        xor r8, r8;
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], r8;              // value = 0 (BOOL)
        lea r11, [rip + flag_ids];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], r15;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_kinds];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], rax;

        add rcx, 1;
        lea r11, [rip + flag_count];
        mov [r11], rcx;

        // Advance to next letter. Cluster stores intentionally skip
        // the ENH-032 (--version) / ENH-014 (--help) auto-emit
        // dispatches — see the ENH-013 preamble for why.
        add rbp, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_cluster_disp_head;

      parse_argv_cluster_disp_done:
        jmp parse_argv_advance_loop;

      parse_argv_positional:
        lea r11, [rip + pos_count];
        mov rax, [r11];
        cmp rax, 32;                        // MAX_POS
        jge parse_argv_pos_overflow;

        lea r11, [rip + pos_ptrs];
        mov [r11 + rax*8], rdx;             // pos_ptrs[m] = argv[i]

        add rax, 1;
        lea r11, [rip + pos_count];
        mov [r11], rax;
        jmp parse_argv_advance_loop;

      parse_argv_advance_loop:
        add r13, 1;
        jmp parse_argv_loop_head;

      parse_argv_long_missing_name:
        mov rax, 5;                         // ERR_LONG_MISSING_NAME
        jmp parse_argv_maybe_collect;

      parse_argv_flag_overflow:
        // ENH-017: storage-exhaustion errors do NOT route through
        // maybe_collect — the flag_names/flag_values arrays are full
        // and every subsequent store would hit the same gate. Route
        // to parse_argv_fail directly; the record path there still
        // populates error_ring[0] if it is empty, and preserves the
        // first-collected error otherwise (see parse_argv_fail).
        mov rax, 1;                         // ERR_FLAG_OVERFLOW
        jmp parse_argv_fail;

      parse_argv_pos_overflow:
        // ENH-017: same rationale as parse_argv_flag_overflow — the
        // pos_ptrs array is full; continuing would trip this gate on
        // every subsequent positional. Direct fail.
        mov rax, 2;                         // ERR_POS_OVERFLOW
        jmp parse_argv_fail;

      parse_argv_missing_value:
        mov rax, 6;                         // ERR_MISSING_VALUE
        jmp parse_argv_maybe_collect;

      parse_argv_unknown_flag:
        mov rax, 12;                        // ERR_UNKNOWN_FLAG (ENH-004)
        jmp parse_argv_maybe_collect;

      parse_argv_unknown_arg_form:
        mov rax, 3;                         // ERR_UNKNOWN_ARG_FORM (ENH-026)
        jmp parse_argv_maybe_collect;

      parse_argv_bad_int:
        // ENH-017 (Closes #27): FKIND_INT flag captured a value that
        // failed Typed::parse_int_u64 decoding. r13 has been advanced
        // to point at the failing value slot by the store block above,
        // so recording (17, r13) attaches the error to the argv index
        // that carried the bad token — a consumer's diagnostic
        // renderer walks `argv[error_at(i).argv_index]` and gets the
        // failing string verbatim. The store itself is NOT rolled
        // back (see ERR_BAD_INT docstring in parsed_args.pdx).
        mov rax, 17;                        // ERR_BAD_INT
        jmp parse_argv_maybe_collect;

      parse_argv_cluster_with_arity:
        mov rax, 16;                        // ERR_CLUSTER_WITH_ARITY
        jmp parse_argv_maybe_collect;

      parse_argv_string_enum:
        // ENH-019 (Closes #29): FKIND_STR flag captured a value
        // that did not byte-equal any entry in the caller-registered
        // allowed set (register_string_enum). r13 has NOT been
        // further advanced beyond the value slot the parser landed
        // on — for the lookahead form (`--color auto`) r13 points
        // at the value's argv slot after the pre-store `add r13, 1`
        // that consumed argv[i+1]; for the inline form
        // (`--color=auto` / `--color:auto`) r13 still points at the
        // combined flag+value slot. Either way, argv[error_arg_index]
        // yields the offending token for a consumer's diagnostic
        // renderer (the inline form's separator was in-place NUL-
        // terminated by parse_argv_name_has_sep, so the flag half
        // reads back as a bare `--color` while the value pointer
        // in flag_values[k] points past that NUL into `auto` — the
        // parser's own state stays coherent). The store itself is
        // NOT rolled back (see ERR_STRING_ENUM docstring in
        // parsed_args.pdx).
        mov rax, 18;                        // ERR_STRING_ENUM
        jmp parse_argv_maybe_collect;

      parse_argv_maybe_collect:
        // ENH-017 (Closes #27) — the recoverable-fail router.
        // Inputs:  rax = err_code, r13 = argv_index
        // Behavior:
        //   1. Record (err_code, argv_index) into
        //      error_ring[error_ring_count] if capacity remains.
        //      If this is the FIRST error (ring_count == 0), also
        //      publish into ParsedArgs::error_code / error_arg_index
        //      so a consumer that never reads the ring still sees the
        //      first failure via the pre-existing scalar slots.
        //   2. Load parse_flags from [rsp]; if bit 0
        //      (ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS) is set, advance to the next
        //      argv slot (jmp parse_argv_advance_loop). Otherwise
        //      fall through to parse_argv_epilogue, which returns
        //      rax (the current err_code, same as the pre-ENH-017
        //      fail-return value).
        //
        // Clobbers: rcx (parse_flags reload / ring_count / bit test),
        //           r11 (lea scratch). rax and r13 preserved.
        //
        // Note: this path is NEVER entered on an overflow-class fail
        // (see parse_argv_flag_overflow / pos_overflow above), so the
        // "advance-past-argv-slot" recovery is always safe — the
        // recoverable ERR_* codes leave the storage arrays in a
        // coherent state, either because no store happened
        // (unknown/missing_name/unknown_flag/cluster_with_arity) or
        // because the store succeeded and only the value was
        // semantically bad (bad_int, string_enum — ENH-019).
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];
        cmp rcx, 16;                        // MAX_COLLECTED_ERRORS
        jge parse_argv_maybe_collect_gate;
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_codes];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], rax;
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_indices];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], r13;
        cmp rcx, 0;
        jne parse_argv_maybe_collect_incr;
        // First error: also publish to the scalar slots.
        lea r11, [rip + error_code];
        mov [r11], rax;
        lea r11, [rip + error_arg_index];
        mov [r11], r13;
      parse_argv_maybe_collect_incr:
        add rcx, 1;
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_count];
        mov [r11], rcx;

      parse_argv_maybe_collect_gate:
        // Test bit 0 of the spilled parse_flags.
        mov rcx, [rsp + 0];
        and rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je parse_argv_epilogue;             // no bit → return rax
        jmp parse_argv_advance_loop;        // bit set → keep parsing

      parse_argv_fail:
        // Hard-fail path (overflow class + auto-emit signals).
        // rax = err_code, r13 = argv_index.
        //
        // ENH-017 (Closes #27) first-error preservation:
        // If the ring already has content (a prior recoverable error
        // captured under collect-all mode), leave error_code /
        // error_arg_index unchanged — they hold the FIRST error's
        // code per the "first error wins" contract. Otherwise this
        // IS the first error (non-collect mode's single error, or an
        // overflow that fired before any recoverable one), so
        // populate error_ring[0] + the scalar slots + set ring_count
        // to 1 so a consumer's error_at(0) walk still finds the
        // hard-stop error.
        //
        // Auto-emit success signals (ENH-032/ENH-014) also route
        // here with rax == 13 / 15 respectively; those codes end up
        // in error_ring[0] alongside the scalar slot writes — which
        // is defensive for a consumer that queries the ring even on
        // a success signal. In collect-all mode with prior
        // recoverable errors, the auto-emit code appends only if
        // capacity remains and lets the epilogue return the FIRST
        // error's code (auto-emit still fires — the fd-1 write
        // already happened before this label).
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];
        cmp rcx, 0;
        jne parse_argv_fail_append;
        // First error via hard-fail: seed the ring + scalar slots.
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_codes];
        mov [r11], rax;
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_indices];
        mov [r11], r13;
        lea r11, [rip + error_code];
        mov [r11], rax;
        lea r11, [rip + error_arg_index];
        mov [r11], r13;
        mov rcx, 1;
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_count];
        mov [r11], rcx;
        jmp parse_argv_epilogue;

      parse_argv_fail_append:
        // Ring already has content — append this hard-fail (or
        // auto-emit signal) if capacity remains, then jump to
        // epilogue which returns the FIRST-error code from ring[0].
        cmp rcx, 16;                        // MAX_COLLECTED_ERRORS
        jge parse_argv_epilogue;
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_codes];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], rax;
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_indices];
        mov [r11 + rcx*8], r13;
        add rcx, 1;
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_count];
        mov [r11], rcx;
        jmp parse_argv_epilogue;

      parse_argv_done_ok:
        // ENH-017 (Closes #27): under collect-all mode the parser
        // may reach end-of-argv with N > 0 recorded errors. In that
        // case error_code was set to the FIRST error's code by
        // parse_argv_maybe_collect; leave it and fall through to
        // the epilogue, which reads error_ring_codes[0] into rax.
        // With ring empty (the pre-ENH-017 success path), clear
        // error_code and return ERR_OK (0) — behavior byte-for-byte
        // identical to the pre-ENH-017 shape.
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];
        cmp rcx, 0;
        jne parse_argv_epilogue;
        xor rax, rax;                       // ERR_OK
        lea r11, [rip + error_code];
        mov [r11], rax;
        // fall through to epilogue

      parse_argv_epilogue:
        // ENH-017 return-value discipline: if the ring has content,
        // return error_ring_codes[0] (first-error code) regardless
        // of the rax value at entry; otherwise return whatever rax
        // is (ERR_OK from done_ok, or the current err_code from a
        // non-collect fail). Consumers see the "first error wins"
        // return-value semantics on every exit path.
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_count];
        mov rcx, [r11];
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je parse_argv_epilogue_pop;
        lea r11, [rip + error_ring_codes];
        mov rax, [r11];

      parse_argv_epilogue_pop:
        add rsp, 8;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      }
    }

  // parse_argv(argv, argc) → u64
  //
  // libpdx-argv.ENH-017 (Closes #27) — thin wrapper over parse_argv_ex
  // that forwards parse_flags = 0, giving every pre-ENH-017 caller
  // byte-for-byte identical behavior: return on first error, no
  // multi-error accumulation, no inline INT validation. The ring
  // still captures the first error (parse_argv_maybe_collect / the
  // parse_argv_fail seed both populate error_ring[0]), so a consumer
  // that opts into the ring API can still walk error_at(0) after a
  // plain parse_argv call — the single-error case just reports
  // error_count() == 1.
  //
  //   rdi = argv (u64* array, unchanged from the pre-ENH-017 shape)
  //   rsi = argc
  //   rax = same as parse_argv_ex — ERR_OK or one of the ERR_* codes.
  //
  // Non-leaf (calls parse_argv_ex). One-push prologue (rbx unused,
  // pushed only for stack alignment) brings rsp%16 to 0 for the
  // nested call, per the same pattern parse_argv_skipping_zero uses.
  // rax survives the pop rbx / ret path (rbx is callee-save and
  // unused; no instruction between the call return and the epilogue
  // touches rax).
  //
  // Register plan:
  //   rdi = argv       (passed straight to parse_argv_ex)
  //   rsi = argc       (passed straight to parse_argv_ex)
  //   rdx = parse_flags = 0 (staged before the call)
  //   rbx = scratch (unused, pushed only for stack alignment)
  //   rax = return value from parse_argv_ex (passed through unchanged)
  pub let parse_argv : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (argv: u64) (argc: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-argv.ENH-017 (Closes #27) Parser::parse_argv — thin wrapper over parse_argv_ex that forwards (rdi=argv, rsi=argc, rdx=0). The pre-ENH-017 signature (u64, u64) -> u64 is preserved so every existing consumer (pkg, ls, cp, mkdir, mv, rm, mkfs.pdxfs, mount.pdxfs, umount.pdxfs, and every satellite/test that links libpdx-argv today) sees no ABI change. parse_flags = 0 means bit 0 (ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS) is unset, so parse_argv_ex's fail paths route through parse_argv_maybe_collect but the gate at parse_argv_maybe_collect_gate falls through to parse_argv_epilogue on the first error — byte-for-byte identical to the pre-ENH-017 return-on-first-error contract, including that no inline FKIND_INT validation happens (the validation is gated on the same bit). Non-leaf: 1-push prologue (rbx unused, for rsp alignment) → rsp%16: 8 → 0 for the nested parse_argv_ex call. rax survives pop rbx / ret unchanged.",
      block: {
        push rbx;                           // align rsp (rsp%16: 8 → 0)
        xor rdx, rdx;                       // parse_flags = 0
        call parse_argv_ex;                 // rax = parse_argv_ex result
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      }
    }

  // parse_argv_skipping_zero(argv, argc) → u64
  //
  // libpdx-argv.ENH-031 (post-1.1.0). Thin wrapper over parse_argv that
  // drops argv[0] — the program-name slot every satellite _start passes
  // per the frozen execve ABI (design/user/execve-abi.md) — before
  // forwarding into the standard parser. Consumers can hand the argv
  // pointer and argc they received at _start unmodified; the wrapper
  // isolates the +1/-1 offset arithmetic every caller would otherwise
  // re-implement (see paideia-satellites/ls src/argv_surface.pdx which
  // silently captured its own program name into pos_ptrs[0] pre-ENH-031).
  //
  //   rdi = argv (u64* array, argv[0] is the program name)
  //   rsi = argc (count of entries in argv, including argv[0])
  //   rax = same as parse_argv (ERR_OK or one of the ERR_* codes).
  //
  // When argc == 0 the call short-circuits to a bare parse_argv on the
  // caller's pointer with count 0 — parse_argv's loop head condition
  // (`cmp r13, r12; jge parse_argv_done_ok`) returns ERR_OK immediately
  // without dereferencing argv, so a nil argv pointer is also safe on
  // this path. The argc == 0 guard is what keeps `add rdi, 8; sub rsi,
  // 1` from advancing past the end of a zero-length argv or wrapping
  // the count to 0xFFFF...FFFF.
  //
  // Non-leaf (calls parse_argv). One-push prologue (rbx unused, pushed
  // purely for alignment) brings rsp%16 to 0 for the nested call, per
  // the same pattern StdVocab::register_all uses for its FlagSpec calls.
  // rax survives the pop rbx / ret path (rbx is callee-save and unused;
  // no instruction between the call return and the epilogue touches rax).
  //
  // Register plan:
  //   rdi = argv (adjusted in-place when argc > 0)
  //   rsi = argc (decremented in-place when argc > 0)
  //   rbx = scratch (unused, pushed only for stack alignment)
  //   rax = return value from parse_argv (passed through unchanged)
  pub let parse_argv_skipping_zero : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (argv: u64) (argc: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-argv.ENH-031 Parser::parse_argv_skipping_zero — thin wrapper over parse_argv that skips argv[0] (the program-name slot every satellite _start passes per the frozen execve ABI). Consumer hands the un-adjusted argv/argc it received at _start; the wrapper advances rdi by 8 (one pointer slot) and decrements rsi by 1, then tail-calls parse_argv. Non-leaf: 1-push prologue (rbx unused, for rsp alignment) → rsp%16: 8 → 0 for the nested parse_argv call. Guards argc==0 by forwarding to parse_argv with the caller's (argv, 0) unchanged — parse_argv's loop-head check (cmp r13, r12; jge done_ok) returns ERR_OK immediately without dereferencing argv, so a zero-length argv (nil pointer included) is safe. Every consumer of libpdx-argv called from _start (pkg, ls, cp, mkdir, mv, rm, mkfs.pdxfs, mount.pdxfs, umount.pdxfs) wants this shape; pre-ENH-031 each caller had to know to pass argv+8, argc-1 or accept the program name landing in pos_ptrs[0]. The wrapper is symmetrically safe on the FlagSpec-registration precondition: it does not touch FlagSpec/ParsedArgs state, so the same parsed_args_reset() + registration sequence documented for parse_argv applies unchanged.",
      block: {
        push rbx;                           // align rsp (rsp%16: 8 → 0)

        // argc == 0 → nothing to skip; fall through to parse_argv on
        // (argv, 0) which returns ERR_OK immediately.
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je parse_argv_skipzero_forward;

        add rdi, 8;                         // argv += 1 pointer slot
        sub rsi, 1;                         // argc -= 1

      parse_argv_skipzero_forward:
        call parse_argv;                    // rax = parse_argv result

        pop rbx;
        ret;
      }
    }

  // parse_argv_ctx(ctx_ptr, argv, argc) → u64
  //
  // libpdx-argv.ENH-006 (Closes #17). Caller-owned ParsedArgsCtx variant
  // of parse_argv. Resets the ParsedArgs singleton, calls parse_argv on
  // the caller's argv, then snapshots the singleton state into the
  // caller's ctx block and returns the parse error code unchanged. The
  // net effect: two consecutive `parse_argv_ctx(&a, …)` /
  // `parse_argv_ctx(&b, …)` calls leave `&a` and `&b` with byte-
  // independent parse results — the multi-parse fingerprint ENH-006
  // exists to deliver (shell needs it for many command lines per
  // process, subshells, pkg subcommand re-parse).
  //
  //   rdi = ctx_ptr (caller-owned [u64; 168] block @align(8); MUST
  //         be non-null — the singleton path is served by the
  //         pre-existing parse_argv entry point, kept unchanged)
  //   rsi = argv    (u64* array, as parse_argv accepts)
  //   rdx = argc    (count of entries in argv)
  //   rax = same as parse_argv — ERR_OK on success, else an ERR_*
  //         code. The code AND the ParsedArgs slots are ALSO published
  //         into ctx via ParsedArgs::pa_ctx_snapshot, so a caller who
  //         only reads through the ctx pointer gets the full result
  //         without touching the singleton.
  //
  // Implicit reset: parse_argv_ctx calls parsed_args_reset before the
  // nested parse_argv, so the caller need NOT reset explicitly between
  // successive parse_argv_ctx calls. This differs from parse_argv
  // (which relies on the caller's own parsed_args_reset) and is
  // deliberate: the multi-parse idiom does not want a bookkeeping
  // dance between every parse.
  //
  // Non-leaf (calls parsed_args_reset + parse_argv + pa_ctx_snapshot).
  // Stack shape:
  //   push rbx           (rsp%16: 8 → 0)  ; rbx = argv survives calls
  //   push r12           (rsp%16: 0 → 8)  ; r12 = ctx_ptr survives calls
  //   sub  rsp, 8        (rsp%16: 8 → 0)  ; argc spill + return-value spill
  //
  //   [rsp + 0] initially holds argc (before parse_argv); after
  //   parse_argv returns it is reused to hold the return value across
  //   the nested pa_ctx_snapshot call.
  //
  // Register plan:
  //   rbx = argv       (callee-save; survives every nested call)
  //   r12 = ctx_ptr    (callee-save; survives every nested call)
  //   [rsp+0] = argc, then parse_argv rax
  //   rax = final return value
  pub let parse_argv_ctx : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (ctx_ptr: u64) (argv: u64) (argc: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-argv.ENH-006 (Closes #17) Parser::parse_argv_ctx — caller-owned ParsedArgsCtx wrapper around parse_argv. Sequence: parsed_args_reset (zero the singleton so a prior parse's residue does not leak), parse_argv(argv, argc) (runs the entire existing parser unmodified, writing into the singleton), pa_ctx_snapshot(ctx) (copies the 13 per-parse slots from singleton into caller's ctx block). Return value = parse_argv's rax, passed through unchanged. The snapshot design keeps ENH-006 additive: the massive parse_argv_ex body (1200+ lines, every ENH-004/013/014/017/019/020/026/032 dispatch inside it) is not touched — every existing consumer sees byte-identical behaviour through the singleton entry points, and the multi-parse fingerprint is delivered by the snapshot-after step. Non-leaf: 2 pushes (rbx=argv, r12=ctx_ptr — both must survive nested calls that clobber SysV caller-save) + sub rsp,8 for argc/rax spill = 24 bytes on stack after ret addr → rsp%16 = 0 at every nested call site (parsed_args_reset, parse_argv, pa_ctx_snapshot). parsed_args_reset takes no arguments (rdi/rsi/rdx unused); parse_argv takes (rdi=argv, rsi=argc); pa_ctx_snapshot takes (rdi=ctx_ptr). The [rsp+0] slot dual-uses as argc-carrier (before parse_argv) and rax-carrier (across pa_ctx_snapshot); each use lifetime is disjoint so no additional stack slot is needed. 3-arg unsafe-bodied lambda well under B1708's 6-param cap.",
      block: {
        push rbx;                             // rsp%16: 8 → 0
        push r12;                             // rsp%16: 0 → 8
        sub rsp, 8;                           // rsp%16: 8 → 0

        mov r12, rdi;                         // r12 = ctx_ptr
        mov rbx, rsi;                         // rbx = argv
        mov [rsp + 0], rdx;                   // spill argc

        call parsed_args_reset;

        mov rdi, rbx;                         // argv
        mov rsi, [rsp + 0];                   // argc
        call parse_argv;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;                   // spill parse return value

        mov rdi, r12;                         // ctx_ptr
        call pa_ctx_snapshot;

        mov rax, [rsp + 0];                   // recover parse return value

        add rsp, 8;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      }
    }
}
